25 claims; 0 removal(s).
  run_ids: ["c9d4392a-0b5a-4ad5-aa2c-b0f75bf2348d"] -> ["72de7e19-ff8c-4d6d-88b2-3bd1364f8f94"]
  explanation: "For rand_pcg 0.10.2, the four state getters return their stored word. The three LCG stream getters return increment>>1; from_state preserves its input state and stores increment=(stream<<1) OR 1, discarding the highest stream bit. Their new constructors initialize state=((input_state+increment)*multiplier+increment) with modular arithmetic; the multipliers are 6364136223846793005, 47026247687942121848144207491837523525 and 15750249268501108917 respectively. Mcg128Xsl64::new stores input_state OR 1. Native output proofs retain Lcg64Xsh32::try_next_u32 and both word widths for the three 128-bit types. The XSL-RR variants permute the newly advanced state; CM-DXSM permutes the old state then advances. Their 32-bit methods return the low 32 bits of the 64-bit result with the same final state. Original Result::map and conversion closures are body-extracted. Mcg128Xsl64::advance additionally terminates normally for every u128 delta and every internal u128 state, returning state=M^delta*initial_state modulo 2^128, M=47026247687942121848144207491837523525. A well-founded halving-loop proof connects the original body to this ordinary power specification, covering all deltas without an artificial execution bound. These twenty-five direct original-body API guarantees apply under default and serde because the selected official translations are byte-identical; the serde implementations themselves remain unproved. Each of the three LCG advance methods also terminates normally for every full-width delta, state and increment, preserving the stored increment and producing exactly delta repetitions of the ordinary modular affine step x↦M*x+increment. An independent linear recurrence specifies this behavior; a generic affine-composition proof connects the original binary algorithm to it. Increments need not be odd for this state-transform identity. Separate Kani evidence calls the unchanged Mcg128Xsl64::from_seed and public state getter for every sixteen-byte seed. Its state equals the independently decoded little-endian u128 seed OR 1 (242 successful checks under each of default and serde). The exact rand_core 0.10.0 and serde 1.0.228 dependency lock is retained. This is a separate Kani body guarantee, not another Aeneas machine claim or a formal cross-tool equivalence proof. Separately, four Kani harnesses call the original manual Debug implementations through all typed inputs of the listed new/from_state/from_seed/seed_from_u64 constructors. Under default formatting and a fixed 64-byte writer that accepts all bytes or rejects the first write, they emit the fixed redacted type-name literal and propagate the writer error. All four baseline rand_core0.10.0 jobs complete with 8925 checks and no failures. For Lcg64Xsh32 with serde, a separate Kani check decodes every 16-byte wire value through a fixed sequence deserializer, including zero and even increments. For successful two-word decoding, public state() equals the first little-endian u64 and stream() equals the second u64 shifted right by one; successful concrete serialization reproduces all sixteen bytes, including the increment low bit, without changing the RNG. Short sequences of length0 or1 return the concrete invalid_length error, and errors at any reached one of five Deserialize callback stages propagate the supplied code." -> "For rand_pcg 0.10.2, the four state getters return their stored word. The three LCG stream getters return increment>>1; from_state preserves its input state and stores increment=(stream<<1) OR 1, discarding the highest stream bit. Their new constructors initialize state=((input_state+increment)*multiplier+increment) with modular arithmetic; the multipliers are 6364136223846793005, 47026247687942121848144207491837523525 and 15750249268501108917 respectively. Mcg128Xsl64::new stores input_state OR 1. Native output proofs retain Lcg64Xsh32::try_next_u32 and both word widths for the three 128-bit types. The XSL-RR variants permute the newly advanced state; CM-DXSM permutes the old state then advances. Their 32-bit methods return the low 32 bits of the 64-bit result with the same final state. Original Result::map and conversion closures are body-extracted. Mcg128Xsl64::advance additionally terminates normally for every u128 delta and every internal u128 state, returning state=M^delta*initial_state modulo 2^128, M=47026247687942121848144207491837523525. A well-founded halving-loop proof connects the original body to this ordinary power specification, covering all deltas without an artificial execution bound. These twenty-five direct original-body API guarantees apply under default and serde because the selected official translations are byte-identical; the serde implementations themselves remain unproved. Each of the three LCG advance methods also terminates normally for every full-width delta, state and increment, preserving the stored increment and producing exactly delta repetitions of the ordinary modular affine step x↦M*x+increment. An independent linear recurrence specifies this behavior; a generic affine-composition proof connects the original binary algorithm to it. Increments need not be odd for this state-transform identity. Separate Kani evidence calls the unchanged Mcg128Xsl64::from_seed and public state getter for every sixteen-byte seed. Its state equals the independently decoded little-endian u128 seed OR 1 (242 successful checks under each of default and serde). The exact rand_core 0.10.0 and serde 1.0.228 dependency lock is retained. This is a separate Kani body guarantee, not another Aeneas machine claim or a formal cross-tool equivalence proof. Separately, four Kani harnesses call the original manual Debug implementations through all typed inputs of the listed new/from_state/from_seed/seed_from_u64 constructors. Under default formatting and a fixed 64-byte writer that accepts all bytes or rejects the first write, they emit the fixed redacted type-name literal and propagate the writer error. All four baseline rand_core0.10.0 jobs complete with 8925 checks and no failures. For Lcg64Xsh32 with serde, a separate Kani check decodes every 16-byte wire value through a fixed sequence deserializer, including zero and even increments. For successful two-word decoding, public state() equals the first little-endian u64 and stream() equals the second u64 shifted right by one; successful concrete serialization reproduces all sixteen bytes, including the increment low bit, without changing the RNG. Short sequences of length0 or1 return the concrete invalid_length error, and errors at any reached one of five Deserialize callback stages propagate the supplied code. For Lcg128Xsl64 (Pcg64) with serde, another concrete sequence check covers every 32-byte wire value, including zero and even increments. Successful two-u128 decoding has public state() equal to the first little-endian u128 and stream() equal to the second shifted right by one; its successful serialization reproduces all32 bytes without changing state. Length0 or1 returns the concrete invalid_length error, and a supplied error at any reached one of five Deserialize callback stages propagates exactly."
  trusted_assumptions: "The Rust compiler, official Charon/Aeneas translation, official Aeneas scalar models and Lean kernel are trusted. No custom external models, source adaptations or local trusted helper contracts are used. All twenty-one API theorem dependency audits permit only propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound. Wrapping arithmetic, shifts, casts and rotations use official standard-library models, rather than proofs of Rust std implementation bodies. The original Result::map body is extracted and proved through its actual closure calls, rather than supplied by a custom model. This serde supplement trusts Kani0.68/CBMC and its August Rust compiler with exact rand_core0.10.0/serde1.0.228. Its concrete sequence backend, invalid_length handler and successful serializer define the observed protocol; they do not stand for arbitrary backends." -> "The Rust compiler, official Charon/Aeneas translation, official Aeneas scalar models and Lean kernel are trusted. No custom external models, source adaptations or local trusted helper contracts are used. All twenty-one API theorem dependency audits permit only propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound. Wrapping arithmetic, shifts, casts and rotations use official standard-library models, rather than proofs of Rust std implementation bodies. The original Result::map body is extracted and proved through its actual closure calls, rather than supplied by a custom model. This serde supplement trusts Kani0.68/CBMC and its August Rust compiler with exact rand_core0.10.0/serde1.0.228. Its concrete sequence backend, invalid_length handler and successful serializer define the observed protocol; they do not stand for arbitrary backends. The Lcg128Xsl64 supplement uses the same exact Kani/Rust/core0.10.0/serde1.0.228 trust boundary and concrete sequence/serializer protocol. Its successful run used unwind40 with unwinding assertions enabled."
  environment: "Unchanged crates.io rand_pcg 0.10.2 Rust sources and Cargo.toml; original archive/hash checks, tests and regeneration comparisons retained in evidence. Rust nightly-2026-09-17; Charon 08d7c9d50144bd84848212c24701253585e90d1f; official Aeneas frontend/library be86ab0f1989d04944292b345b24c435be3ad10a; Lean 4.35.0. Official targeted extraction includes each actual original body, macro-expanded state helpers and private constructor helpers. Separate all-features (serde) extraction is retained in evidence/serde; generated Lean is byte-identical to the default extraction. Six additional output bodies and core::result::Result::map are included via the official --include core::result selection. Two output-permutation helper theorems are audited separately. The MCG advance extraction uses the official PcgAdvanceModel namespace. Its full-delta power/termination proof uses BitVec algebra and a decreasing natural measure, and all configured proof modules are imported together for the final audit. Three LCG advance bodies use a separate official PcgAffineAdvanceModel namespace. BitVec affine algebra and halving induction prove all deltas with increment preservation; all configured proof modules are imported together. Separate Debug supplement: Kani0.68.0/Rust nightly-2026-08-21, rand_core0.10.0 exact lock, four jobs/8925 checks; evidence/kani-debug-baseline. The distinct0.10.1 execution packet is not substituted for this baseline. Separate Lcg64Xsh32 serde Kani0.68/nightly2026-08-21, exact core0.10.0/serde1.0.228/default+serde:691checks/0fail/1unreachable. evidence/kani-pcg32-serde; only dependency path adapted for portable artifact layout. All prior25 proofs/MCGseed/Debug evidence retained." -> "Unchanged crates.io rand_pcg 0.10.2 Rust sources and Cargo.toml; original archive/hash checks, tests and regeneration comparisons retained in evidence. Rust nightly-2026-09-17; Charon 08d7c9d50144bd84848212c24701253585e90d1f; official Aeneas frontend/library be86ab0f1989